  "dom_id": "step-q348716",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039635/assets/a2ccde221d71e611f7917d6dac0d12ca6b8204d9cd80a21d79005195245cf77a.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u20612\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{x}{3}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+3\\operatorname{cos}\u20610 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
